PART D  The k_{g,f} datum (Sec 2.3 'strict functor carrying the k_{g,f}'; Rem 11 '[g] is its k-invariant')
============================================================================================
On C=B(Z/2): alpha strict (alpha_g^2=id) forces k_{g,g} in Z. Cocycle law k_{h,gf} alpha_h(k_{g,f}) = k_{hg,f} k_{h,g}
holds for any k_{g,g}=z in Z (Aut fixes the central involution). Neutrality of the AUT(F)-pseudofunctor (alpha,k):
  exists mu in Aut K, T_g in K with c_{T_g} = mu alpha_g mu^-1 and T_{gg}=e = mu(k_{g,g}) T_g T_g.
D2 (Def 6) is the same with k ignored (T_g^2 = e).
  n= 4 alpha=id            D2:True  | true neutrality k=e:True  k=z:False
  n= 4 alpha=c_r           D2:False | true neutrality k=e:False k=z:True 
  n= 4 alpha=c_{r^(n/4)}   D2:False | true neutrality k=e:False k=z:True 
  n= 6 alpha=id            D2:True  | true neutrality k=e:True  k=z:False
  n= 6 alpha=c_s           D2:True  | true neutrality k=e:True  k=z:False
  n= 8 alpha=id            D2:True  | true neutrality k=e:True  k=z:False
  n= 8 alpha=c_{r^(n/4)}   D2:False | true neutrality k=e:False k=z:True 
  n=10 alpha=id            D2:True  | true neutrality k=e:True  k=z:False
  n=10 alpha=c_s           D2:True  | true neutrality k=e:True  k=z:False
  n=12 alpha=id            D2:True  | true neutrality k=e:True  k=z:False
  n=12 alpha=c_{r^(n/4)}   D2:False | true neutrality k=e:False k=z:True 
  => with k=z central the same strict alpha flips: Thm 22(ii)'s witness (n=4, c_r) becomes neutral, and alpha=id
     becomes non-neutral for EVERY even n (incl. n=6). Under the pseudofunctor reading the criterion is 2|n, not 4|n.
